nd see what's revealed  in particular what did they find they  said that our simulated attacks  demonstrated that depending on the  quality of the external data used  between 52 and 175 million respondents  in the 2010 census can be correctly  re-identified from the reconstructed  micro data  and  this had big shock waves and immediately  they kind of pivoted the policy of how  they're going to be publishing data in  20 census and hereafter  another recent announcement related to  this privacy protection issue is that  they're going to discretize certain  variables that they release such as  wages  now wages are really important for  economists  and  some have already documented what this  might do so there's something called the  atlanta fed wage tracker the fed is  part of the federal reserve system  and they track wages over time  they have these plots of wage growth  that people use to inform policy  you can see the original data series  before thinking about how discretization  will be implemented and it's this kind  of blue slash green series  but if you implement the discretization  they've proposed you get this red series  so you can see it's very corrupted and  this is very worrisome  there was a big backlash and actually  the the census bureau announced that  they will delay this policy until maybe  inflation abates or something like that  so what does this mean for causal  inference  [Music]  social scientists are worried about  these  new types of data corruption of course  to people here at simon's these might  not sound new like differential privacy  and discretization  but even before the 2020 census  these data sets had kind of old or  classical types of data corruption which  are very much studied in causal  inference such as missing values and  measurement error  now what's the standard practice what's  the standard workflow for let's say an  economist trying to measure  a treatment effect from one of these  data sets  well they'll do some data cleaning and  then they'll do some data analysis that  kind of ignores the bias and variance  consequences of the data cleaning  in particular what are those  consequences roughly speaking well when  you clean a data set you tend to make it  more homogeneous and correlated than it  originally was  and in the effort to rein in the bias  from different types of data corruption  you've probably also really depleted the  variance  existing confidence intervals ignore  this phenomenon and in simulations you  can show that typically they'll be too  small too optimistic coverage will be  about 70 instead of the 95 you'd expect  so what's the kind of solution that we  would like  well  we would ideally have a solution that  estimates causal parameters as if the  data were uncorrupted  yet still adjusts for data cleaning in  the confidence interval  we want this procedure to not require  knowledge of the corruption covariance  structure as well  if such a procedure existed it would in  some sense preempt this looming  trade-off between privacy and protection  sorry privacy and precision  that is faced by social scientists and  other researchers who use these data  sets  this is maybe the most important slide  why is this hard  i think that several challenges arise  and here are just a few of them first of  all  when we justify confidence intervals  we typically  appeal to a central limit theorem that  requires independence or at least weak  dependence among observations  meanwhile  data cleaning in general induces really  strong dependence across observations  that you need and even if you think  about it across every single entry  the next  issue is that if we think of the data  cleaning literature perhaps return to  the matrix completion literature we see  results that at if we want relatively  good rates  are for recovering not  matrix entries  uh entry wise or in max norm but rather  kind of matrix entries on averages  across rows and columns a fabrinius norm  type of idea 